WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.240
Season 2 of The Mandalorian was an&nbsp;
Easter egg-filled extravaganza — one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.920
that had many Star Wars fans leaping&nbsp;
for joy. From the return of Boba&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:13.200
Fett to the live-action debut of Clone Wars&nbsp;
favorites such as Bo-Katan and Ahsoka Tano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.840
the second season of The Mandalorian somehow&nbsp;
cleared the high bar set by the first. But if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.160
you ask many fans about the biggest surprise&nbsp;
moment the second season had to offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.960
they'll probably mention a certain cloaked&nbsp;
Jedi who makes an appearance in the finale.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.680
In "Chapter 16, The Rescue," the Mandalorian&nbsp;
and his allies are pitted against a legion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.560
Dark Troopers, and all hope looks lost until&nbsp;
a young Luke Skywalker arrives on the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.040
brings down the evil droids, and takes&nbsp;
Grogu off for some special Jedi training.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.840
It was easily the series' biggest moment thus&nbsp;
far, with Skywalker's youthful appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.520
coming down to some unique technology&nbsp;
and a little collaboration with Mark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.520
Hamill — the "Star Wars" legend who is&nbsp;
also a self professed fan of the series.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:01:01.219
"It's very much in the spirit of what George&nbsp;
wanted originally, and I just thought it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.219 --> 00:01:02.800
superb."

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.120
Still, although many fans loved this moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:10.320
others were less impressed — mostly by the&nbsp;
execution of Skywalker's de-aging process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.560
Either way, we're about to learn more about the&nbsp;
technology that went into this cameo moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.920
thanks to an upcoming Disney+&nbsp;
special centered around it.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.400
Mandalorian fans may have already tuned into&nbsp;
the documentary series Disney Gallery: The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.840
Mandalorian, which offers a behind-the-scenes look&nbsp;
at individual episodes of the show, and provides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.400
crucial information about how each individual&nbsp;
story — and the series as a whole — came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.520
life. Now that Season 2 has run its course, most&nbsp;
fans assumed that The Mandalorian's Disney Gallery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.400
series was unlikely to return until the main&nbsp;
show's third season. Those same people might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.800
be surprised to learn that the House of Mouse&nbsp;
is bringing back its popular behind-the-scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.920
docuseries well ahead of schedule, with a&nbsp;
fresh episode set to hit Disney+ in August.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.480
Disney+ will premiere their special&nbsp;
episode of Disney Gallery on August 25th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:02.400
2021, which focuses on the return of Luke&nbsp;
Skywalker in the Mandalorian Season 2 finale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.280
Among other things, the episode will reveal more&nbsp;
about the type of technology used to resurrect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.160
Luke in his classic Return of the Jedi look, as&nbsp;
well as examine the collaborative process with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.880
Mark Hamill that proved essential to making&nbsp;
Luke's appearance as realistic as possible.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.960
So, if you were one of the many Star Wars fans&nbsp;
who got a little teary-eyed at the return of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.440
everyone's favorite Jedi master, then this new&nbsp;
episode of Disney Gallery will be right up your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.640
alley. But what can we actually expect to learn&nbsp;
from it? Well, aside from knowing that actor Max&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.280
Lloyd-Jones was responsible for the bodywork&nbsp;
of young Luke, there's precious little anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.160
does know about the technical and collaborative&nbsp;
work that went into Luke's return. It's possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.720
to make a reasonable guess how it all worked,&nbsp;
considering similar technology has been used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.520
in previous Star Wars movies and elsewhere&nbsp;
in Hollywood, but nothing is certain here;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.640
outside of the production, nobody's even sure how&nbsp;
much involvement Hamill even had in the process.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.080
But while many fans will be thrilled&nbsp;
to get some answers to those questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.800
others might be less than thrilled to return&nbsp;
to the Mandalorian Season 2 finale. Indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.560
while the moment of Luke's triumphant&nbsp;
return garnered much praise from many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.360
some would have preferred that Disney recast&nbsp;
the role instead of using de-aging technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:16.480
Online Star Wars fans even had a consensus&nbsp;
candidate to fill young Skywalker's Jedi boots:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:22.640
MCU mainstay Sebastian Stan. Twitter user&nbsp;
@djesson81 summed up the most emphatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.680
faction of the fandom's thoughts after the&nbsp;
Mandalorian Season 2 finale aired, tweeting:

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.720
"Jesus Christ, just use Sebastian Stan for Luke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.560
rather than this janky PS3&nbsp;
[cutscene] looking version we got!"

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.320
Of course, it'll be interesting to hear&nbsp;
whether anybody ever considered recasting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.080
Luke for his special appearance in The&nbsp;
Mandalorian — or whether it was de-aging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.160
tech all the way for Jon Favreau and company.&nbsp;
Whether they did or not, though, we're bound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.880
to know for certain when the newest episode&nbsp;
of Disney Gallery hits Disney+ this August.

